
the board directory contains some board independent source files and
board support packages (BSPs), which are board dependent.

the board independent files are made available as a "board-library" for
applications that use the board independent code to do something.  one example
is firmware flasher tools which need to use some of the board functions to
build the ROM image or something similar.

each BSP is located in a sub directory:

board   // this directory
 |
 +--  mcb_v1     // MCB v1 BSP
 |
 +--  my_board   // another BSP
         |
         +-- include   // added as include directory for the compiler as
         |             // the very first place to look for includes.
         |             // this can override system and toolchain includes.
         |
         +-- toolchain_additions.cmake  // if this file is present it will be
                                        // included into the toolchain file.
                                        // this can define additional build
                                        // targets etc.


when configuring an app build, one specifies the toolchain file (and directory),
the board directory for the target BSP and the libraries directory.

if the board directory is specified, doing

  import_library (board)

in an app's CMakeLists.txt will include the board library of the BSP.

if the board directory is not specified, the BSP independent version of the
board library will be included.


example cmake line:

cmake \
-DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=~/code/toolchain/rx/toolchain.cmake \
-DCMAKE_BOARD_DIR=~/code/board/mcb_v2 \
-DBOARD_VARIANT=RX71M_176 \
-DCMAKE_LIBRARIES_DIR=~/code \
~/code/my_app
